-**Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Precision:** Monocular depth estimation errors can propagate into geometry supervision [S2].
-*   **Boundaries:** The method assumes the reference view is correctly inpainted; errors in $I_r$ affect the entire 3D result [S2].
-*   **Disocclusions:** Regions visible in non-reference views but occluded in the reference view require specific handling via $L_{do}$ [S2].
 
 ## Subtopics
 
 ### MVInpainter
-A multi-view consistent inpainting model designed to bridge 2D and 3D scene editing [S1]. It employs domain adapters and motion modules as video priors to enhance cross-view consistency [S1]. It introduces a Reference Key&Value concatenation technique for appearance consistency while incorporating slot attention to aggregate optical flow features for controlling camera movement without explicit pose conditions [S1].
+MVInpainter is a multi-view consistent inpainting model designed to bridge 2D and 3D scene editing [S1]. It employs domain adapters and motion modules as video priors to enhance cross-view consistency [S1]. It introduces a Reference Key&Value concatenation technique for appearance consistency while incorporating slot attention to aggregate optical flow features for controlling camera movement without explicit pose conditions [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-*   **Time Complexity:** Depends on the number of iterations and the cost of monocular depth estimation and bilateral solver.
-*   **Memory Complexity:** Standard NeRF memory plus storage for reference view and intermediate disparity maps.
-*   **Strengths:** Avoids view inconsistencies by using a single reference view; generates view-dependent effects; controllable via a single image [S2].
-*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the single reference view; may struggle with complex disocclusions [S2].
-*   **When to Use:** When a user has a single edited image and wants to propagate it consistently across all views of a NeRF [S2].
-*   **When Not to Use:** When multi-view consistency is critical and multiple edited views are available, or when the reference view is unreliable [S1, S2].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method requires iterative optimization of the NeRF. The complexity is dominated by the number of iterations $N_{depth}, N_{bilateral}, N_{do}$ and the cost of monocular depth estimation and bilateral solving per iteration.
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Requires storing the NeRF parameters, input images, masks, and intermediate depth maps.
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids view inconsistencies by relying on a single reference view; generates view-dependent effects; allows user control via a single image [S2].
+*   **Limitations:** Relies on the quality of the single inpainted reference view; may struggle with complex geometry and textures in disoccluded regions [S1, S2].
+*   **When to use:** When a user wants to inpaint a NeRF with specific content provided as a single 2D image and requires view-consistent results [S2].
+*   **When not to use:** When high-quality multi-view inpainted inputs are available and consistency is less critical than texture fidelity, or when the reference view is of poor quality [S2].
 
 ## Background
-Originated from the work on Reference-guided Controllable Inpainting of Neural Radiance Fields [S2]. It addresses the challenge of 3D scene inpainting by leveraging a single reference view, avoiding the need for multi-view edited inputs [S2].
+Reference-guided Controllable Inpainting of NeRFs was introduced in 2023 [S2]. It addresses limitations in existing scene inpainting methods, such as inaccurate removal of large objects and lack of multi-view consistency [S1]. The method builds on the principles of differentiable volume rendering and NeRF optimization [Parent].
 
